      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-213430",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={2,4,8}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(2,4),(2,8),(4,2),(4,4),(4,8)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nIn this relation, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y){{field-1}}R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z){{field-2}}R\\,\u27f9\\,{{field-3}}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is \n{{field-4}}.\n\nFor example, since $(2,4)$ and $(4,8)$ belong to $R,$ transitivity of $R$ requires \n{{field-5}}\n$\\in R.$"
    },
